Background. When Lattice renders a deck, its export pipeline embeds
engine assets into the exported artifact: the engine and theme CSS in
exported HTML decks, and the preview/runtime JavaScript inlined into
fluid HTML exports. Without this exception, conveying such a deck (for
example, posting it on a web server) could be read as conveying a
covered work, imposing AGPL conditions on the deck's author.

Grant. As a special exception, you may convey the engine assets that
Lattice's export pipeline embeds into a rendered deck — the CSS
bundles, theme CSS, and runtime JavaScript, as embedded by Lattice
itself — as part of that rendered deck, under terms of your choice,
without the AGPL-3.0 applying to the rendered deck as a whole and
without any obligation to offer Corresponding Source for those
embedded assets.

Limits. This exception applies only to engine assets that Lattice's
own export pipeline embeds into a rendered deck. It does not extend
to: (a) the Lattice engine itself or any of its source or object form
distributed other than inside a rendered deck; (b) the embedded assets
extracted or separated from a rendered deck for reuse; or (c) any
modified version of the engine or of the embedded assets beyond the
mechanical embedding the export pipeline performs. The content of your
deck — your text, images, and data — was never covered by the AGPL in
the first place and needs no exception.

If you convey a rendered deck, you may (but need not) retain this
notice in it. Removing this exception from a copy of Lattice is
permitted by AGPL-3.0 section 7, but copies distributed by SlideWright
carry it.
